 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tours_Amphitheatre]; Equivalently, the cathedral stands in front of the Roman amphitheatre (cathedral in front, amphitheatre behind) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tours_Amphitheatre]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('67215100-355e-4586-ae41-00838c8e1566'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'Megalithic Greek temple in Sicily dedicated since 550 BC to a local form of Artemis, worshipped here as Diana. Used for nearly a thousand years, blending pre-Greek megalithic and classical traditions.', 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:67215100-355e-4586-ae41-00838c8e1566:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description ''Megalithic Greek temple in Sicily dedicated since 550 BC to a local form of Artemis, worshipped here as Diana. Used for nearly a thousand years, blending pre-Greek megalithic and classical traditions.'' restored on Temple of Diana, Cefalù, Sicily', '||e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: no-description", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "no-description"}]'::jsonb),
    ('673332a5-3a27-4045-a929-ddbf7ff7e824'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, '5th century BC acropolis on Thasos with stone walls, an amphitheatre, a castle, and a temple to Athena. What survives is fragmentary — restoration preserves what remains of this hilltop sanctuary.', 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:673332a5-3a27-4045-a929-ddbf7ff7e824:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description ''5th century BC acropolis on Thasos with stone walls, an amphitheatre, a castle, and a temple to Athena. What survives is fragmentary — restoration preserves what remains of this hilltop sanctuary.'' restored on The Acropolis of Ancient Thasos', '||e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: no-description", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "no-description"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6880170a-df11-4dbf-8c6c-02aa4e39ec7b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Clunia, an ancient Roman city in present-day Feldkirch, Vorarlberg, Austria, is marked on the Tabula Peutingeriana as a settlement along the Roman road network. The map still names it.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:6880170a-df11-4dbf-8c6c-02aa4e39ec7b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Clunia, Austria', 'e0cd4078e8ee5d887d6a3ba3e60da61f3c8250f1c554f3cf9cd42a92d58098eb|68fc657a2c7e5203cc24938c3dd37aeea9fa200606b863873070ab2bc2fb1088|68fc657a2c7e5203cc24938c3dd37aeea9fa200606b863873070ab2bc2fb1088', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Clunia, an ancient Roman city in present-day Feldkirch, Vorarlberg, Austria, is marked on the Tabula Peutingeriana as a settlement along the Roman road network. The map still names it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Clunia was an ancient Roman city [S1]; it is in present-day Feldkirch, Vorarlberg, Austria [S1]; it is marked on the Tabula Peutingeriana as a settlement along the Roman road network [S1]; the map still names it [S1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Clunia is an ancient Roman city (a Roman settlement) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clunia,_Austria]; It is at Feldkirch, in the Feldkirch area of Vorarlberg, Austria [SUPPORTED: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clunia_(Feldkirch)]; The Tabula Peutingeriana is an ancient Roman road map [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tabula_Peutingeriana]; Clunia lay at a road station on the Roman road network, in Austria [SUPPORTED: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clunia_(Feldkirch)]; Clunia is recorded/indicated on the Tabula Peutingeriana [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clunia,_Austria]; The map still names it today (that the ancient map still carries the name) [UNVERIFIABLE, no proving quote: None]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('68859b06-2135-4711-a160-da003d18ccca'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'Two Beinan culture standing stones, 5.75 m and 3.99 m tall, raised 2,000-3,000 years ago within an oval site 600 m long. Their purpose remains unknown.', 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:68859b06-2135-4711-a160-da003d18ccca:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description ''Two Beinan culture standing stones, 5.75 m and 3.99 m tall, raised 2,000-3,000 years ago within an oval site 600 m long. Their purpose remains unknown.'' restored on Saoba Stone Pillars', '||e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: no-description", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "no-description"}]'::jsonb),
    ('68c4944b-9e3a-4561-955c-33fa9978fc86'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1940 a cranium nearly 15,000 years old came to light at the Keilor Archaeological Site. Hearths about 31,000 years BP; megafauna remains suggest a possible Aboriginal hunting link.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:68c4944b-9e3a-4561-955c-33fa9978fc86:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Keilor Archaeological Site', '299c5f38b631ed8ff79ad9675045bf37f9a6d8e12365a63c53f1f93663529ea4|c9bb5d3510a9b86771e652943ea3053d4a22e332cd0bf5bd5e92f93df2e22742|c9bb5d3510a9b86771e652943ea3053d4a22e332cd0bf5bd5e92f93df2e22742', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-rewrite1-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1940 a cranium nearly 15,000 years old came to light at the Keilor Archaeological Site. Hearths about 31,000 years BP; megafauna remains suggest a possible Aboriginal hunting link."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check1-002 (check1), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "in 1940 a cranium came to light at the site [S1]; the cranium is nearly 15,000 years old [S1]; hearths about 31,000 years BP [S2]; megafauna remains suggest a possible association with Aboriginal hunting [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The site is an archaeological site in Victoria, Australia (Keilor archaeological site) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keilor_archaeological_site]; A cranium came to light / was found at the site in 1940 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keilor_archaeological_site]; The cranium was nearly 15,000 years old [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keilor_archaeological_site]; Hearths about 31,000 years BP were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keilor_archaeological_site]; Megafauna remains suggest a possible Aboriginal hunting association [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keilor_archaeological_site]; The nature and extent of human/megafauna interaction remains unresolved (the hunting link is only possible, not established) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keilor_archaeological_site]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('696fa141-d76e-4882-ab59-75f8a0d46d47'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Built in the Ionic order, the colonnade of the Garni Temple fell in the earthquake of June 4, 1679 and was rebuilt from its own stones, without mortar, in grey to bluish basalt.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:696fa141-d76e-4882-ab59-75f8a0d46d47:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Garni Temple', 'f34c73b3ec6b84ad3c4a6f4ef3f92e9721222153f5ebb1a8b872c29c06d952db|cabc51ac30d7eb354af6d64e4d5add4ef406c7f591656b343a26562159fd6e4c|cabc51ac30d7eb354af6d64e4d5add4ef406c7f591656b343a26562159fd6e4c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Built in the Ionic order, the colonnade of the Garni Temple fell in the earthquake of June 4, 1679 and was rebuilt from its own stones, without mortar, in grey to bluish basalt."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the temple was built in the Ionic order [S2]; the colonnade fell in the earthquake of June 4, 1679 [S6]; it was rebuilt using its original stones [S7]; the structure is assembled without mortar [S8]; it is constructed of grey to bluish basalt [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-026 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Garni Temple is built in the Ionic order (its colonnade is Ionic) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garni_Temple]; The colonnade fell / collapsed in the earthquake of June 4, 1679 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garni_Temple]; The earthquake happened on June 4, 1679 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garni_Temple]; The temple was rebuilt/reconstructed (1969-75, using the anastylosis technique) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garni_Temple]; The temple is built of locally quarried grey to bluish basalt [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garni_Temple]; The stone blocks were assembled without the use of mortar [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garni_Temple]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6a199307-b761-4214-82da-2dbd4c89832c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On an isolated 80-metre hilltop, Cannington Camp is a univallate hillfort occupied from the Mesolithic through Roman periods. It is proposed as the site of the 878 AD Battle of Cynuit.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:6a199307-b761-4214-82da-2dbd4c89832c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Cannington Camp', 'c33591c436efcf99e4486b6d8d80bd06cfc3d6f40c09532a5d5b2758db8d7aeb|1fd4b08caa4107e26315dce4641237afa84fa8bf6cb881fb0eba93199c6590a0|1fd4b08caa4107e26315dce4641237afa84fa8bf6cb881fb0eba93199c6590a0', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On an isolated 80-metre hilltop, Cannington Camp is a univallate hillfort occupied from the Mesolithic through Roman periods. It is proposed as the site of the 878 AD Battle of Cynuit."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cannington Camp is a univallate hillfort [S1]; it is on an isolated 80-metre hilltop [S1]; it was occupied from the Mesolithic through Roman periods [S2]; it has been proposed as the site of the 878 AD Battle of Cynuit [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cannington Camp is a univallate hillfort, that is a hill fort with a single rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannington_Camp]; Cannington Camp is a Bronze Age and Iron Age hill fort near Cannington, Somerset, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannington_Camp]; The hill on which the fort stands rises to 80 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannington_Camp]; The hilltop on which the fort stands is isolated [UNVERIFIABLE, no proving quote: None]; Mesolithic occupation is attested on or near the hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannington_Camp]; Bronze Age finds from the site include an axe head and a knife [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannington_Camp]; The hillfort was in use during the Roman period [SUPPORTED: https://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/som/vol6/pp73-76]; Cannington Camp is proposed (possibly) as the site of the Battle of Cynuit/Cynwit of 878 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cannington_Camp]; The identification is only a proposal: the location of the 878 battle is not known with certainty [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Cynwit]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6a43a3e7-66c7-4133-a91c-58a52cc5e0dc'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'A Roman bridge spanning a gorge the legion called "Hercules'' Kick." The gorge is only 40 m wide but its walls rise 120 m—a gateway to the desert.', 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:6a43a3e7-66c7-4133-a91c-58a52cc5e0dc:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description ''A Roman bridge spanning a gorge the legion called "Hercules\'' Kick." The gorge is only 40 m wide but its walls rise 120 m—a gateway to the desert.'' restored on The Roman Bridge (Elguentra)', '||e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: no-description", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "no-description"}]'::jsonb),
    ('6a4cdd7d-42a8-4952-a1b9-7d6bb44f090a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The terraces of Quriwayrachina, an archaeological site also called Quri Wayrachina, step across Peru''s Cusco Region in Anta Province, about 4 km north of the terraces of Zurite.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:6a4cdd7d-42a8-4952-a1b9-7d6bb44f090a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Quriwayrachina, Anta', 'ae5f616f6285c44acfd81845976af5afb1e409eb597daa443c80bfd7541c13cf|68ec8567d58b43faf91e46442772474a6841d1a44c6bea3659a6b3507413dd8b|68ec8567d58b43faf91e46442772474a6841d1a44c6bea3659a6b3507413dd8b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-009", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The terraces of Quriwayrachina, an archaeological site also called Quri Wayrachina, step across Peru''s Cusco Region in Anta Province, about 4 km north of the terraces of Zurite."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-009 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Quriwayrachina is an archaeological site with agricultural terraces [S1]; it is also called Quri Wayrachina [S1]; it is in the Cusco Region of Peru, Anta Province [S1]; it lies about 4 km north of the terraces of Zurite [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-027 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Quriwayrachina, Anta is an archaeological site with agricultural terraces in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quriwayrachina,_Anta]; The site is also called Quri Wayrachina [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quriwayrachina,_Anta]; The site is situated in the Cusco Region of Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quriwayrachina,_Anta]; The site is in Anta Province [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qur