    ('75f2454d-5538-42ea-87b2-ba091dec3df6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Searching for a Roman road in 1984, the Trent and Peak Archaeological Trust uncovered two Early Neolithic longhouses at Lismore Fields, among the best-preserved in Britain.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:75f2454d-5538-42ea-87b2-ba091dec3df6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Lismore Fields', '472d12d1843bfbc548139dc6cb7f512f687d589c9a502578902e5a546ea64a91|1ce66801970c215313edf0f9dc20551c97da01c836d6ee455faa76212a8a4296|1ce66801970c215313edf0f9dc20551c97da01c836d6ee455faa76212a8a4296', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Searching for a Roman road in 1984, the Trent and Peak Archaeological Trust uncovered two Early Neolithic longhouses at Lismore Fields, among the best-preserved in Britain."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site was discovered in 1984 by the Trent and Peak Archaeological Trust [S1]; the discovery was made while searching for a Roman road [S1]; excavations revealed two Early Neolithic longhouses at the site [S2]; the longhouses are among the best-preserved examples in Britain [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Lismore Fields is the site of a Stone Age settlement (prehistoric settlement) at Buxton, Derbyshire. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lismore_Fields]; The settlement was discovered in 1984. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lismore_Fields]; The uncovering was done by the Trent and Peak Archaeological Trust. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lismore_Fields]; The search that found it was a search for a Roman road. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lismore_Fields]; Excavation uncovered the remains of two Neolithic longhouses. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lismore_Fields]; The card dates the longhouses to the Early Neolithic specifically; no page I could open gives a Neolithic sub-period for them. [UNVERIFIABLE, no proving quote: None]; The longhouses are among the best-preserved in Britain. [SUPPORTED: https://www.lismorefields.com/]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('76bd4687-787b-48d8-bbb5-e0d063bbf4c6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Colebrooke, Devon, the agger of a Roman road still shows in a field roughly 300 metres south of Rag Lane. It fades near the village, yet a straight lane along the ridge suggests its line.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:76bd4687-787b-48d8-bbb5-e0d063bbf4c6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Colebrooke, Devon', '89ad52dd734ac3f52b6a58a37513453967ac1f858816f3b960b470af4227d2f7|0e135ed552d38b846dfff1346d04ae7973a9420d348d1a91d5537aff157d0351|0e135ed552d38b846dfff1346d04ae7973a9420d348d1a91d5537aff157d0351', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Colebrooke, Devon, the agger of a Roman road still shows in a field roughly 300 metres south of Rag Lane. It fades near the village, yet a straight lane along the ridge suggests its line."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Colebrooke is a village in Devon [S1]; remains of a Roman road agger are visible in a field roughly 300 metres south of Rag Lane [S2]; the road becomes less distinct at Colebrooke [S3]; a straight lane along the ridge suggests the road''s original line [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The place is Colebrooke, a village and parish in Devon, England. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colebrooke%2C_Devon]; At Colebrooke the agger of the Roman road (the page''s ''Roman road'' section) is still visible in a field. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colebrooke%2C_Devon]; The agger lies roughly 300 metres south of Rag Lane. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colebrooke%2C_Devon]; The road''s route becomes less obvious (fades) at that point near the village. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colebrooke%2C_Devon]; A rather straight lane along the ridge is suggestive of the road''s line. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colebrooke%2C_Devon]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('77c8e01e-c8c8-4667-99f2-e36c81d6c0f8'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'Six defensive wall systems built around Rome from the 8th century BC to the 17th century AD. The earliest circuits are still largely traceable — 2,500 years of fortification layered in stone.', 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:77c8e01e-c8c8-4667-99f2-e36c81d6c0f8:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description ''Six defensive wall systems built around Rome from the 8th century BC to the 17th century AD. The earliest circuits are still largely traceable — 2,500 years of fortification layered in stone.'' restored on Roman Walls', '||e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: no-description", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "no-description"}]'::jsonb),
    ('79132373-7a68-45e3-a174-3b1e429a12b3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The northernmost point of Spain, Punta da Estaca de Bares reaches into the Cantabrian Sea and takes more than 2500 mm of rain a year, one of the rainiest places in Europe.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:79132373-7a68-45e3-a174-3b1e429a12b3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Punta de Estaca de Bares', '39e1dc61fc51b637237f3bb0d19f0f87af41b59c02336b1e2644beb52cc13301|efb27e6a18427e66579e8fb88480365a850f1cf532b9178b5a86d045335b57bd|efb27e6a18427e66579e8fb88480365a850f1cf532b9178b5a86d045335b57bd', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The northernmost point of Spain, Punta da Estaca de Bares reaches into the Cantabrian Sea and takes more than 2500 mm of rain a year, one of the rainiest places in Europe."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Punta da Estaca de Bares is the northernmost point of Spain [S1]; the peninsula penetrates into the Cantabrian Sea [S3]; it receives more than 2500 mm of rain a year [S3]; it is one of the rainiest places in Europe [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-029 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Punta de Estaca de Bares is the northernmost point of Spain. [SUPPORTED: https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estaca_de_Bares]; The cape reaches into the Cantabrian Sea (it is the dividing point between the Cantabrian Sea and the Atlantic). [SUPPORTED: https://gl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estaca_de_Bares]; The cape takes more than 2500 mm of rain a year. [UNVERIFIABLE, no proving quote: None]; It is one of the rainiest places in Europe. [SUPPORTED: https://pt.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estaca_de_Bares]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7cfc73a7-180b-4378-a14a-785e9cab28a4'::uuid, 'card_description', 'About 3 km south of Manfredonia, Siponto was a port town and bishopric of Magna Graecia. In 189 BC it became the Roman colony Sipontum; in 663 AD the Slavs took and destroyed it.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:7cfc73a7-180b-4378-a14a-785e9cab28a4:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Siponto', '06bea2c8f75ce42221e5c66c8974c63b709f01cd55139b2b07bbbbcbd940cf9c|9c30943decd70affc214cb4eae0f359003b8300933a8e98167fc3c0aed69caf7|9c30943decd70affc214cb4eae0f359003b8300933a8e98167fc3c0aed69caf7', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "About 3 km south of Manfredonia, Siponto was a port town and bishopric of Magna Graecia. In 189 BC it became the Roman colony Sipontum; in 663 AD the Slavs took and destroyed it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Siponto lies about 3 km south of Manfredonia [S2]; Siponto was an ancient port town and bishopric of Magna Graecia [S1]; in 189 BC it became a Roman colony with the name Sipontum [S3]; in 663 AD the Slavs took and destroyed it [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Siponto was an ancient port town and bishopric of Magna Graecia in southern Italy (Apulia) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siponto]; Siponto lay in Magna Graecia in Apulia, southern Italy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siponto]; Siponto is located about 3 km south of Manfredonia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siponto]; In 189 BC Siponto became the Roman colony Sipontum [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siponto]; In 663 AD Siponto was taken and destroyed by the Slavs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siponto]; Siponto was a bishopric (a bishopric of Sipontum was established around 400 AD) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siponto]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('7e88e3ce-568c-4cea-aa71-79713b290df1'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near the village of Berel in eastern Kazakhstan, Berel Kurgan holds numerous 5th-3rd century BCE Early Saka kurgans, vast quantities of golden jewelry, and horses buried beside their owners.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:7e88e3ce-568c-4cea-aa71-79713b290df1:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Berel Kurgan', '7a0405a777bb22b72171667336cdafceb7f65a76c467c7b661948ce97f32a936|d4cc57e388ea1c10ec59367ef8dda731aa6d1cd72cc8322f0d515f576a1bafc8|d4cc57e388ea1c10ec59367ef8dda731aa6d1cd72cc8322f0d515f576a1bafc8', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near the village of Berel in eastern Kazakhstan, Berel Kurgan holds numerous 5th-3rd century BCE Early Saka kurgans, vast quantities of golden jewelry, and horses buried beside their owners."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is in eastern Kazakhstan [S1]; the site is near the village of Berel [S2]; numerous 5th-3rd century BCE Early Saka kurgans were found at the site [S3]; the kurgans contained vast quantities of golden jewelry [S4]; horses were buried in the kurgans next to their owner [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Berel Kurgan is an archaeological site in the Katonkaragay District of eastern Kazakhstan, located near the village of Berel [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berel_kurgan]; Numerous kurgans were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berel_kurgan]; The kurgans date to the 5th-3rd century BCE and belong to the Early Saka [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berel_kurgan]; The kurgans contained vast quantities of golden jewelry [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berel_kurgan]; Horses were buried in the kurgans next to their owners [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berel_kurgan]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('81724be4-6588-41b1-8efc-364637456cdf'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Cut into the upper slopes of Whitehorse Hill in Oxfordshire, the Uffington White Horse runs 110 m in trenches of crushed white chalk, made between 1380 and 550 BC.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s033-rollback:81724be4-6588-41b1-8efc-364637456cdf:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s033: card_description None restored on Uffington White Horse', '8a50525b2e73f1c4b6340004e641cf1202a679714fa94be2189ae77554acbdc4|a599d7655c16257dbf7822482579633734fa1d3f5273526e30f911d7f6b07515|a599d7655c16257dbf7822482579633734fa1d3f5273526e30f911d7f6b07515', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: writer teaser-write-010", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cut into the upper slopes of Whitehorse Hill in Oxfordshire, the Uffington White Horse runs 110 m in trenches of crushed white chalk, made between 1380 and 550 BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: checker teaser-check-010 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the figure is cut into the upper slopes of Whitehorse Hill in Oxfordshire [S1, S2]; the figure is 110 m long [S1]; it is formed from trenches of crushed white chalk [S1]; it was created between 1380 and 550 BC [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-cardgap-2026-10-07: verifier teaser-verify-030 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-cardgap-2026-10-07/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Uffington White Horse is a prehistoric hill figure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uffington_White_Horse]; The figure is 110 m long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uffington_White_Horse]; It is formed from deep trenches filled with crushed white chalk [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uffington_White_Horse]; The figure is situated on the upper slopes of Whitehorse Hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uffington_White_Horse]; Whitehors